## Add the button

The resource contract names the operation. The command captures its target, so the component does not choose an endpoint or manage an optimistic rollback.


```tsx
import type { ActionHandle } from "@hyecompany/vem-data";
import { useAction, useCommand } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/react";
import { useState } from "react";
import { type TaskRef, tasks } from "../task-board/contract.js";

export function CompleteTask({ target }: { target: TaskRef }) {
	const complete = useCommand(tasks.commands.complete(target));
	const [action, setAction] = useState<ActionHandle>();
	if (action) return <ActionFeedback action={action} onRetry={setAction} />;
	return (
		<div>
			<button
				type="button"
				disabled={!complete.canRun}
				onClick={() => setAction(complete.run())}
			>
				Complete task
			</button>
			{complete.availability.reason && <p>{complete.availability.reason}</p>}
		</div>
	);
}
```


`canRun` means Vem can admit an attempt **now**. A known denial disables the button and supplies a reason. Unknown authorization still permits an attempt; the server can reject it. Admission is checked again when you run.

This completion button keeps the returned handle so it can show feedback in place of the button. Holding a handle observes shared work; it does not create a second operation registry.

## Show progress and the result

`ActionFeedback` uses `useAction` from the same imports above:


```tsx
export function ActionFeedback({
	action,
	onRetry,
}: {
	action: ActionHandle;
	onRetry: (action: ActionHandle) => void;
}) {
	const state = useAction(action);
	const [controlError, setControlError] = useState<string>();
	const outcome = state.outcome;
	const message = !outcome
		? (state.progress?.message ?? "Working…")
		: outcome.kind === "succeeded"
			? "Task completed."
			: outcome.kind === "failed"
				? outcome.error.message
				: outcome.kind === "partial"
					? (outcome.message ?? "Only part of the change was saved.")
					: outcome.message;
	return (
		<div>
			<output>{message}</output>
			{controlError && <p role="alert">{controlError}</p>}
			{state.controls.canRetry && (
				<button type="button" onClick={() => onRetry(action.retry())}>
					Try again
				</button>
			)}
			{state.controls.canCancel && (
				<button
					type="button"
					onClick={async () => {
						try {
							await action.cancel();
						} catch {
							setControlError(
								"Cancellation could not be confirmed. Check the operation before trying again.",
							);
						}
					}}
				>
					Cancel operation
				</button>
			)}
		</div>
	);
}
```


`run()` returns immediately. `useAction` observes the action as it progresses; imperative code can `await action.result`. A domain can supply more specific success or partial values for the feature to render.

| Result | Meaning for your UI |
| --- | --- |
| `succeeded` | The operation completed according to the available evidence |
| `partial` | Some work succeeded; describe that work and what remains |
| `failed` | A failure is known; it may have happened after submission |
| `unknown` | There is not enough evidence to say what happened |

An unknown result is not permission to retry. Show retry only when `controls.canRetry` permits it, and cancellation only when `controls.canCancel` permits it. A partial save must not become “nothing was saved.”

## Let other views follow the change

The definition supplies proposals and confirmed updates. A list using `useQuery` receives them automatically. The feature does not invalidate queries or restore an old snapshot on failure.

### A proposal sits above confirmed facts

1. The service last confirmed this task is open.
2. The component previews completion while the action runs.
3. Events can change the confirmed task beneath the preview.
4. When the preview ends, show current facts, not an old snapshot.

Removing a proposal does not restore a saved old snapshot. Success must be supported by backend evidence; a partial or unknown outcome cannot be rewritten as complete success.

Known updates can arrive while a proposal is visible. Ending that proposal reveals the latest confirmed information. An unknown outcome keeps its proposal labeled unconfirmed; a failed create proposal can also remain for feedback according to the definition.

<details>
<summary>Navigation, feedback, and cancellation</summary>

Removing this component stops its observation. It does not cancel accepted server work. A query's `activities` and `useActivities()` can display shared progress after remounting; the [application feedback layer](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/feedback) handles unsolicited toasts when the feature leaves the screen.

Dismissing a toast acknowledges feedback. Canceling asks the domain to cancel work. A destructive operation such as force-stopping a service is a separate command. These controls should have different labels.

</details>

Edit the notes, then try a teammate’s update before saving. Your input should remain yours to resolve.

### Experiment: Keep your own draft

Change the meeting time and save the task.

A managed edit keeps your input with the version it came from. A teammate’s change does not replace your draft. Saving against that older version conflicts. Read-only access keeps readable input; losing notes access removes just the private notes.

Runs a deterministic simulated source with the real Vem runtime. Interactive controls are available in the HTML page; no JavaScript is needed to read this explanation.

## Bind inputs to the managed draft

The `tasks.edits.notes(target)` definition supplies the saved values and the version used for conditional writes. Your component owns the form:


```tsx
import { useEdit } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/react";
import { type TaskRef, tasks } from "../task-board/contract.js";

export function NotesEditor({ target }: { target: TaskRef }) {
	const edit = useEdit(tasks.edits.notes(target));
	const conflict =
		edit.outcome?.kind === "failed" && edit.outcome.error.code === "conflict";
	return (
		<form
			onSubmit={(event) => {
				event.preventDefault();
				if (edit.canSave) edit.save();
			}}
		>
			{Object.hasOwn(edit.values, "notes") && (
				<label>
					Notes
					<textarea
						value={edit.values.notes ?? ""}
						readOnly={edit.status !== "ready"}
						onChange={(event) => edit.set("notes", event.target.value)}
					/>
				</label>
			)}
			{edit.status === "loading" && <p>Reading the saved notes…</p>}
			{edit.reason && <p aria-live="polite">{edit.reason}</p>}
			{edit.outcome?.kind === "failed" && (
				<p role="alert">
					{conflict
						? "The saved notes changed. Your draft is still here."
						: edit.outcome.error.message}
				</p>
			)}
			{edit.outcome?.kind === "unknown" && (
				<p role="alert">{edit.outcome.message}</p>
			)}
			{edit.outcome?.kind === "succeeded" && <output>Saved.</output>}
			<button type="submit" disabled={!edit.canSave}>
				{edit.saving ? "Saving…" : "Save notes"}
			</button>
			<button
				type="button"
				disabled={edit.saving || edit.status === "loading"}
				onClick={() => void edit.reload()}
			>
				Discard draft and reload
			</button>
		</form>
	);
}
```


`edit.set` updates the draft. `edit.save` creates the save action. `canSave` accounts for preparation, dirty state, an active save, and current write access. Add your own validation alongside it.

`Object.hasOwn` is intentional: readable notes can be an empty string, while an unreadable field is removed from `edit.values`. Do not replace an absent protected field with an editable blank input.

## Handle a newer saved version

An **ETag** is the backend’s token for the version you edited. Saving sends your draft with that original token. If someone saved a newer version, this task definition reports `failed` with error code `conflict`.

The hook keeps the draft and disables another save until you establish a new baseline. The example offers **Discard draft and reload**, explicitly naming the loss of input. A richer editor can offer a comparison UI; Vem does not automatically merge text.

### An editor keeps its concurrency baseline

1. Read the saved notes and their version together.
2. Keep typed changes with that original version.
3. A teammate’s update changes shared facts, not dirty input.
4. Send draft + original version. A conflict preserves the draft.

Reload explicitly discards the draft and prepares a new baseline. Known read revocation removes protected values; write-only loss keeps readable values and disables saving.

A successful save prepares a fresh baseline. A partial or uncertain save requires review; it must not be presented as a clean success. Add rendering for any partial outcome declared by your edit contract.

## Keep readable input; remove unreadable input

Write-only loss disables saving while retaining values the user may still read. Losing read access removes the affected baseline and draft fields. Regaining access does not revive the old private draft; reload an authorized baseline.

The component above renders directly from `edit.values`. Copying protected values into `useState`, logs, or form-library defaults makes extra copies that Vem cannot withdraw. If you adopt a form library, its integration must clear both values and default values when fields are withdrawn.

Defining the backend side belongs in [conditional edits](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/commands-edits), not in each form.

A failed reload preserves the permitted draft and reports an error. Unrelated updates do not clear that error or re-enable saving. Retry the reload to establish a writable baseline; access withdrawal still removes unreadable fields while the error is present.

Use the application's existing `DataClient` in a controller or another UI framework. It describes the same information as a React hook; it does not create another owner.

## Read once


```ts
import type { DataClient } from "@hyecompany/vem-data";
import { type TaskRef, tasks } from "../task-board/contract.js";

export async function readTitle(
	client: DataClient,
	target: TaskRef,
	signal: AbortSignal,
) {
	const result = await client.read(tasks.get(target, { select: ["title"] }), {
		signal,
	});
	return {
		title: result.rows[0]?.data.title,
		exists: result.exists,
		issues: result.issues,
	};
}
```


The read settles with available, partial, or failed information after the acquisition attempt. A missing title remains `undefined`; inspect `issues` rather than assuming a complete object. `exists === false` is established absence.

Pass an `AbortSignal` when the caller needs a deadline or can leave the screen. Aborting, closing the client, or using an invalid description can reject the promise; handle those errors at your caller boundary. There is no automatic deadline for an unresponsive source.

## Run an operation


```ts
export async function completeTask(client: DataClient, target: TaskRef) {
	const action = client.run(tasks.commands.complete(target));
	return await action.result;
}
```


The returned outcome has the same success, partial, failure, and uncertainty meanings as [React actions](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/build/actions). The action handle also provides `subscribe` and `getSnapshot` for progress. Unsubscribing stops observation, not execution.

## Keep a view current

Call this once when the view attaches, and call its returned cleanup when the view leaves:


```ts
export function observeTitles(
	client: DataClient,
	render: (titles: readonly (string | undefined)[]) => void,
) {
	const handle = client.watch(tasks.list({ select: ["title"] }));
	const update = () =>
		render(handle.getSnapshot().rows.map((row) => row.data.title));
	const unsubscribe = handle.subscribe(update);
	update();
	return () => {
		unsubscribe();
		handle.dispose();
	};
}
```


Retry is on the query handle. You do not need a separate refresh query or an event subscription in the view.

Pass `{ acquire: false }` as the second argument to `client.watch` to observe permitted knowledge without requesting missing or stale data. The handle still receives changes and access withdrawal; its explicit `retry()` can acquire.

## Observe read progress without requesting data


```ts
export function observeNoteReads(
	client: DataClient,
	render: (pending: boolean) => void,
) {
	const handle = client.watchReadStatus(tasks.list({ select: ["notes"] }));
	const update = () => render(handle.getSnapshot().pending);
	const unsubscribe = handle.subscribe(update);
	update();
	return () => {
		unsubscribe();
		handle.dispose();
	};
}
```


`watchReadStatus` reports related work from current consumers, including other tabs. Its snapshot has `pending`, `revalidating`, `issues`, and `canRetry`; `handle.retry()` retries unsuccessful matching work. It creates no data demand, and disposing it stops observation without stopping other consumers' reads. See [related read status](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/build/display#show-progress-for-related-reads) for matching and retry behavior.

<details>
<summary>Writing an editor without React</summary>

`prepareEdit` returns the writable values, matching ETag, and disclosure information. `saveEdit` accepts that prepared representation and the draft. A custom editor must manage the access lifetime of copied values, preserve the original baseline while dirty, and discard it on authority or owner changes. The React `useEdit` binding already supplies that state management; the lower-level pair is not a drop-in form binding.

</details>

A feature test should prove what a person sees and can do. `createTestClient` supplies the same provider contract as the application, using named queries, commands, and edits.

## Render an incomplete list

Pass your React test library's `render` function to this helper:


```tsx
import { DataProvider } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/react";
import { createTestClient } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/testing";
import type { ReactNode } from "react";
import { tasks } from "../task-board/contract.js";
import { TaskList } from "./list.js";

export function renderPartialList(render: (view: ReactNode) => void) {
	const fixture = createTestClient({
		queries: [
			{
				query: tasks.list({ select: ["title", "status"], where: undefined }),
				rows: [
					{ identity: { id: "walk" }, data: { title: "Plan a weekend walk" } },
				],
				complete: true,
			},
		],
	});
	render(
		<DataProvider client={fixture.client}>
			<TaskList />
		</DataProvider>,
	);
	return fixture;
}
```


Assert that **Plan a weekend walk** is visible with **Status unavailable**. The collection has complete membership, but the row is missing a display field. The component should not hide it or claim the list is empty.

Unmount the view before calling `fixture.close()` in test cleanup. Keep one fixture per test.

## Change the situation while the view is open


```tsx
export function showFailure(fixture: ReturnType<typeof createTestClient>) {
	fixture.setQuery({
		query: tasks.list({ select: ["title", "status"], where: undefined }),
		rows: [
			{ identity: { id: "walk" }, data: { title: "Plan a weekend walk" } },
		],
		status: "failed",
		error: "The task service is unavailable.",
	});
}
```


Call `showFailure(fixture)` after rendering. Assert that the known task remains visible and the source error is shown. Use `setQuery` to supply another result; `replace` lists fields whose old values must be removed when intentionally switching to a partial scenario.

Fixtures match query descriptions. If a test changes the filter, supply a fixture for that filtered query too, or establish sufficient known information for the runtime to answer it.

## Exercise a conflict and access loss


```tsx
export function conflictFixture() {
	const edit = tasks.edits.notes({ id: "walk" });
	const fixture = createTestClient({
		edits: [
			{
				edit,
				values: { notes: "Meet at 10 by the river." },
				etag: '"version-1"',
				outcome: {
					kind: "failed",
					submitted: true,
					error: { code: "conflict", message: "The saved notes changed." },
				},
			},
		],
	});
	return { fixture, edit };
}
```


Render `NotesEditor` inside `DataProvider` using `fixture.client` and target `{ id: "walk" }`. Type a new meeting time and save. Assert that the conflict appears and the typed value remains.

Then withdraw just the notes:


```tsx
export function hideNotes({
	fixture,
	edit,
}: ReturnType<typeof conflictFixture>) {
	fixture.setEditAccess(edit, { fields: { notes: "denied" } });
}
```


Assert that the textarea and its value disappear. For write-only loss, use `setEditAccess(edit, { write: "denied" })` and assert that Save is disabled while readable input stays.

## Choose the boundary to test

| Change | Useful check |
| --- | --- |
| A field or filter | Type-check selected fields; render missing values |
| A command | Admission, progress, and command-specific outcomes |
| An editor | Dirty external change, conflict, and access loss |
| A domain definition | Real backend responses, events, and replacement boundaries |
| A browser host | Actual worker ownership, two tabs, and storage failures |

Feature fixtures exercise the runtime's consumer behavior, not HTTP, IndexedDB, or worker ownership. Those belong in integration tests. For manual exploration inside an application, see [development tools](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/devtools).

A feature asks for tasks. Your integration describes the available backend reads and what each response provides. Vem chooses among them for the whole application’s outstanding needs.

This guide assumes a task API with two endpoints: `GET /api/tasks` returns **every task and all its fields**; `GET /api/tasks/:id` returns one task. A backend with pagination or brief list representations needs different coverage rules, explained below.

## Give features a contract

The contract names the fields, identity, commands, and editors that components can use. Constructing any of these descriptions performs no work.


```ts
import {
	defineCommand,
	defineEdit,
	defineResource,
} from "@hyecompany/vem-data";

export interface Task {
	title: string;
	status: "open" | "done";
	notes: string;
}
export interface TaskRef {
	id: string;
}
export interface Notes {
	notes: string;
}
export const tasks = defineResource<Task, TaskRef>()({
	name: "tasks",
	fields: ["title", "status", "notes"],
	defaultSelect: ["title", "status"],
	commands: { complete: defineCommand<void, { kind: "succeeded" }>() },
	edits: { notes: defineEdit<Notes, { kind: "succeeded" }>() },
});
```


The command and editor names are declarations; [their implementations](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/commands-edits) come after the read adapter. Keep the contract in a module features can import. The HTTP adapter belongs in the owner, where credentials and transport are available.

## Separate read rules from transport

The task example needs only a name and a public read permission:


```ts
import type { ResourceReadDefinition } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/client";

export const taskRead: ResourceReadDefinition = {
	name: "tasks",
	access: "public",
};
```


Here `ResourceReadDefinition` is imported from `@hyecompany/vem-data/client`. Install these same pure rules in the owner and each tab. Vem evaluates typed `where` conditions, including their required fields when deciding whether a row matches. [Text and combined filters](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/build/display#filter-text) need no custom matcher.

<details>
<summary>Project scopes and advanced matching</summary>

Add `inScope` when a collection has project or tenant boundaries. A resource identity must distinguish otherwise identical names in different projects.

Most resources need no custom matcher. If your integration overrides equality matching with `matches`, it receives normalized equality maps, including equivalent `$and` conditions. List additional fields it reads in `queryDependencies` and return `"unknown"` when those inputs are absent. Vem evaluates the remaining normalized filter expressions itself.

</details>

## Declare sources and extract their evidence

Describe each representation once. `fields` identifies its information; `membership: true` says a list can establish which tasks exist. Vem chooses when to call `run`:


```ts
import type { ResourceDefinition } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
import type { TaskRef } from "../task-board/contract.js";
import { taskRead } from "../task-board/read.js";

export const taskAdapter: ResourceDefinition = {
	...taskRead,
	sources: [
		{
			key: "all-tasks",
			mode: "list",
			fields: ["title", "status", "notes"],
			membership: true,
			async run(_request, context) {
				const records: TaskRecord[] = await readTasks("", context.signal);
				return {
					facts: records.map(taskFact),
					memberships: [
						{
							resource: "tasks",
							identities: records.map(({ id }) => ({ id })),
							complete: true,
							basis: ["public"],
						},
					],
				};
			},
		},
		{
			key: "one-task",
			mode: "one",
			fields: ["title", "status", "notes"],
			async run(request, context) {
				const path = `/${encodeURIComponent((request.identity as TaskRef).id)}`;
				const record: TaskRecord = await readTasks(path, context.signal);
				return { facts: [taskFact(record)] };
			},
		},
	],
};
```


Each `key` names a backend source within this resource. It is not a query cache key; components describe their needs without defining keys.

This complete owner definition imports the contract and read rules above. `TaskRef` is `{ id: string }`; `TaskRecord` is the backend response with that ID plus the contract's task fields. The cast at the adapter boundary uses this resource's identity type. Validate external response shapes in your transport when they are not already checked.

The transport checks HTTP success, and `taskFact` extracts one response:


```ts
import type { FactObservation } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
import type { Task } from "../task-board/contract.js";

interface TaskRecord extends Task {
	id: string;
}
async function readTasks(path: string, signal: AbortSignal) {
	const response = await fetch(`/api/tasks${path}`, { signal });
	if (!response.ok)
		throw new Error(`Task service returned HTTP ${response.status}`);
	return response.json();
}
function taskFact(task: TaskRecord): FactObservation {
	return {
		resource: "tasks",
		identity: { id: task.id },
		values: { title: task.title, status: task.status, notes: task.notes },
		presence: "present",
		basis: ["public"],
	};
}
```


Returning a batch lets the runtime apply it through the acquisition's context. For an intermediate update, use `context.observe(batch)`. Do not start a fetch and later call a global `runtime.observe`: that loses the request's protection against obsolete responses.

| Your response establishes… | Report… |
| --- | --- |
| Some fields of a task | A fact with those `values` |
| The tasks seen on one page | Membership with `complete: false` |
| The entire matching collection | Membership with `complete: true`, for its actual scope and filter |
| One task's details | Its facts; no claim about the whole collection |

Missing display fields keep a known task visible. Unknown filter fields keep a candidate unresolved unless other evidence, such as exact filtered membership, establishes the match. If a successful response cannot provide everything, return what it does establish; reads settle partial. Throw on an acquisition failure so the result can explain it and offer retry.

<details>
<summary>When omission means deletion</summary>

`replace` names a representation's fields whose omission means removal. A brief list response must not delete notes that only appear in details. Report `presence: "absent"` only when absence is established; a concealed resource or generic permission error does not prove deletion.

</details>

## Install the definition

Give `taskAdapter` to the owner's `resources` array and `taskRead` to the tab's read definitions. The [browser-host guide](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/browser-host) shows that connection. Components continue importing `tasks` from the contract; adding a field does not add a fetch effect.

The [automatic planning guide](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/planning) explains how these declarations combine across views and tabs, including costs, filters, and pages. For live updates, continue to [events](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/events). For protected data, [connect permissions](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/access).

If the backend announces that a task is done, every view can use that fact immediately. The adapter translates the event once; components do not subscribe to the event stream or invalidate their queries.

## Publish a value from an event

Add this function as `event: taskStatusEvent` on the task's owner definition. It accepts a small event with an ID and the new status:


```ts
import type { ResourceDefinition } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";

export const taskStatusEvent: ResourceDefinition["event"] = (value) => {
	if (
		typeof value !== "object" ||
		value === null ||
		!("id" in value) ||
		typeof value.id !== "string" ||
		!("status" in value) ||
		(value.status !== "open" && value.status !== "done")
	) {
		throw new Error("Invalid task status event");
	}
	return {
		facts: [
			{
				resource: "tasks",
				identity: { id: value.id },
				values: { status: value.status },
				basis: ["public"],
				liveness: { channel: "task-status" },
			},
		],
	};
};
```


`ResourceDefinition` comes from `@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime`. The validator rejects malformed input. It publishes only the status: it does not pretend this event supplied the task's title, notes, or a complete list.

In the owner, route the transport's decoded messages to that definition:


```ts
import type { Runtime } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";

export function connectedTaskEvents(runtime: Runtime) {
	runtime.setContinuity({ channel: "task-status" }, "live");
}
export function receiveTaskStatus(runtime: Runtime, payload: unknown) {
	runtime.event("tasks", payload);
}
export function lostTaskEvents(runtime: Runtime) {
	runtime.setContinuity({ channel: "task-status" }, "interrupted");
}
```


`Runtime` is imported from the same entry point. Connect `receiveTaskStatus` to decoded events. Call `connectedTaskEvents` only when delivery is continuous for that channel, and `lostTaskEvents` on an interruption. A channel is a named continuity guarantee, not merely an open socket. If a subscription covers only one project, include that project in the channel reference's `scope`.

| The event contains… | The adapter should… |
| --- | --- |
| A new field value | Publish that fact directly |
| Proof a resource was deleted | Publish `presence: "absent"` |
| Only “this field changed” | Mark that specific evidence unsupported so an active reader can acquire it |
| An unknown span of missed changes | Mark the affected channel and scope interrupted |

The last two cases acknowledge missing evidence. They are not a reason to periodically reread ordinary data. An update event cannot prove that an enumeration is complete.

## Declare which fields the events keep current

### Experiment: Different fields, different guarantees

Go offline, change the service, then reconnect. Keep the reply pending, return an error, or confirm the new title.

The real runtime receives an immutable task ID, a title supported by a scoped event channel, and a temperature snapshot. Losing continuity makes the title stale and triggers an unsuccessful targeted read while the simulated service is offline. A service change does not silently update the disconnected view. Reconnecting starts a held check while the old title stays visible. The user can return an error: the title remains last known and the checking indication stops. Retrying and accepting the reply establishes the new title. The ID stays immutable and the old temperature remains a snapshot without automatic polling.

Runs a deterministic simulated source with the real Vem runtime. Interactive controls are available in the HTML page; no JavaScript is needed to read this explanation.

Suppose this backend emits every task-status change, but no title or notes changes. Describe that difference on each acquired representation:


```ts
import type { FactObservation } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
import type { TaskRecord } from "../task-board/domain.js";

export function taskWithStatusEvents(task: TaskRecord): FactObservation {
	return {
		resource: "tasks",
		identity: { id: task.id },
		values: { title: task.title, status: task.status, notes: task.notes },
		presence: "present",
		basis: ["public"],
		fieldLiveness: { status: { channel: "task-status" } },
	};
}
```


Use this extraction in the task sources when the status subscription is installed. `fieldLiveness` overrides the observation's default for those fields. For directly observed values, unlisted fields remain snapshots: reading status events does not make notes current.

| Observation declares… | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `liveness: "snapshot"`, or omitted | The value was observed; ongoing change coverage is not established |
| `liveness: { channel, scope? }` | This value relies on that channel's continuous delivery |
| `liveness: "immutable"` | The field genuinely cannot change for this identity |

Use `{ all: [...] }` when several channels are needed, or `{ any: [...] }` for independently sufficient coverage. These expressions describe continuity; permission expressions answer a separate question.

<details>
<summary>When events reveal some changes but cannot cover every change</summary>

Use `liveness: { all: [{ channel: "task-events" }, "snapshot"] }`. The snapshot requirement prevents a false `live` claim, while the channel still records useful change coverage. A known interruption makes affected evidence stale because an event may have been missed. Plain `"snapshot"` declares no such channel dependency.

</details>

A real interruption makes facts that rely on that channel stale. Unrelated fields and independently sufficient coverage survive. Reads made during the interruption still provide useful snapshots. Reconnecting does not retroactively cover missed changes: affected active consumers acquire new evidence. An unchanged, unmonitored snapshot does not gain a TTL or a mount/focus verification policy.

Keep permitted values on screen during repair. `result.revalidating` signals an active check of stale evidence; `result.stale` remains true if the check stops or fails. [Read presentation](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/build/partial-results#keep-values-visible-while-checking-them) shows the shared status component.

The runtime exposes `row.fields.status?.liveness` as `"live"`, `"snapshot"`, or `"immutable"`; `stale` separately reports known missing currency evidence. `live` applies to that field's declared support. It is not a claim that every field or project is monitored.

## Follow a proven rename

A name can be reused. When the backend explicitly connects an old identity to a new one, publish that connection as identity-transition evidence alongside any values the event establishes.

This example uses `ObservationBatch` from `@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime`. The host must have established the `projects:events` disclosure permission and continuous `project-events` delivery.


```ts
export function projectRenamed(
	oldName: string,
	name: string,
): ObservationBatch {
	return {
		identityTransitions: [
			{
				resource: "projects",
				from: { name: oldName },
				to: { name },
				basis: ["projects:events"],
				liveness: { channel: "project-events" },
			},
		],
		facts: [
			{
				resource: "projects",
				identity: { name },
				values: { name },
				basis: ["projects:events"],
				liveness: { channel: "project-events" },
			},
		],
	};
}
```


The transition atomically establishes the old identity's absence and the new identity's presence. It does not copy field values, grants, actions, or edit sessions. Reading the destination still uses its own access requirements.

A detail result exposes `incarnation`, an opaque token for the identity currently known under that name, and `identityTransitions`, explicit connections from former incarnations. The transition remains available when the old identity has no result row.

Capture the token when the user selects a resource. A shared navigation boundary can follow only a transition matching that captured token:


```ts
export function followSelectedProject(
	selected: { identity: ProjectRef; incarnation: string },
	result: QueryResult<{ name: string }, ProjectRef>,
) {
	// Capture selected.incarnation from the row or detail when the user selects it.
	const transition = result.identityTransitions?.find(
		(entry) => entry.from.incarnation === selected.incarnation,
	);
	return transition?.to ?? selected;
}
```


Here `QueryResult` comes from `@hyecompany/vem-data`, and `ProjectRef` is `{ name: string }`. The boundary owns selection; the picker just displays it. Rename chains resolve to the last supported destination.

**Do not match by name alone.** A newly created project with an old name has a different token. Missing events or withdrawn evidence can also end identity continuity. Keep the selected scope and report uncertainty when no matching proof survives; never infer a rename from list differences.

Transition history is bounded and session-local, not a permanent alias or a persisted redirect. A fresh selection observes the current identity rather than replaying former renames. An action already created for an old reference remains targeted there.

## Derive a value from known facts

Suppose every screen uses a human-readable status label. Define it once instead of storing an unrelated copy:


```ts
export const deriveTaskLabel: ResourceDefinition["derive"] = (
	changed,
	context,
) => ({
	facts: changed
		.filter(
			(ref) =>
				"field" in ref && ref.resource === "tasks" && ref.field === "status",
		)
		.flatMap((ref) => {
			const task = context.get("tasks", ref.identity);
			if (!task || !Object.hasOwn(task.values, "status")) return [];
			return [
				{
					resource: "tasks",
					identity: ref.identity,
					values: {
						statusLabel: task.values.status === "done" ? "Completed" : "To do",
					},
					dependsOn: [ref],
				},
			];
		}),
});
```


Install this as `derive: deriveTaskLabel`. Add `statusLabel` to the public contract before selecting it in a component. `changed` identifies evidence that changed; `context.get` reads what is now known. `dependsOn` connects the derived label to its source status, so withdrawal or invalidation of that support affects the label too.

To let Vem acquire the inputs when a consumer asks for the derived field, declare its prerequisites:


```ts
export const taskDependencies: ResourceDefinition["fieldDependencies"] = {
	statusLabel: (identity) => [
		{
			kind: "query",
			resource: "tasks",
			mode: "one",
			identity,
			select: ["status"],
		},
	],
	labelCount: (identity) => [
		{ resource: "tasks", identity, relationship: "labels" },
	],
};
```


Install this as `fieldDependencies: taskDependencies`. The `labelCount` entry is used by the relationship example below. These callbacks describe required information; Vem selects the sources and shares their work with other consumers.

A derived field automatically inherits the currency of every required `dependsOn` fact and relationship. An explicit liveness requirement can add support requirements, but cannot make an unsupported dependency current.

For ordinary formatting used by only one component, a local formatting function is enough. A domain derivation is useful when multiple readers need the same meaning or it participates in queries.

## Record a relationship when links matter

A task's labels are links to label resources, separate from the labels' names or colors:


```ts
import type { ObservationBatch } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
import type { TaskRef } from "../task-board/contract.js";

export function taskLabels(
	task: TaskRef,
	labelIds: readonly string[],
): ObservationBatch {
	return {
		relationships: [
			{
				resource: "tasks",
				identity: task,
				relationship: "labels",
				targets: labelIds.map((id) => ({
					resource: "labels",
					identity: { id },
				})),
				complete: true,
				basis: ["public"],
			},
		],
	};
}
```


Here `TaskRef` is `{ id: string }`; `ObservationBatch` comes from `@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime`. Return this batch from acquisition or an event definition. Set `complete: true` only if `labelIds` is the full set. A partial response uses `false`; explicit removals use `removed`.

<details>
<summary>When individual links have different permissions</summary>

Suppose a task links to labels that require different read permissions. Use `targetBasis` to protect each link separately. This example assumes those permissions were verified and the response establishes the entire visible set:


```ts
export function protectedTaskLabels(
	task: TaskRef,
	labels: readonly { id: string; readScope: string }[],
	removedLabelIds: readonly string[] = [],
): ObservationBatch {
	const target = (id: string) => ({ resource: "labels", identity: { id } });
	return {
		relationships: [
			{
				resource: "tasks",
				identity: task,
				relationship: "labels",
				targets: labels.map((label) => target(label.id)),
				targetBasis: labels.map((label) => ({
					target: target(label.id),
					basis: [{ all: ["tasks:read-labels", label.readScope] }],
				})),
				removed: removedLabelIds.map(target),
				complete: true,
				basis: ["tasks:read-labels"],
			},
		],
	};
}
```


Withdrawing one label’s permission removes that link and the claim that the set is complete; other permitted links survive. A count or other value derived from the complete set loses its supporting evidence too. If you also publish all label IDs as one scalar array, protect that aggregate with every permission it requires.

A complete response replaces claims with the same disclosure basis; independently supported links can remain. Populate `removedLabelIds` only when the backend establishes those links were removed. `removed` withdraws the link itself, including independent claims; a partial response or a concealed target does not establish removal.

</details>

To expose the number of labels as an ordinary selected field, derive it only when the set is complete:


```ts
export const deriveLabelCount: ResourceDefinition["derive"] = (
	changed,
	context,
) => ({
	facts: changed.flatMap((ref) => {
		if (
			!("relationship" in ref) ||
			ref.resource !== "tasks" ||
			ref.relationship !== "labels"
		)
			return [];
		const labels = context.relationship("tasks", ref.identity, "labels");
		if (!labels?.complete || labels.stale) return [];
		return [
			{
				resource: "tasks",
				identity: ref.identity,
				values: { labelCount: labels.targets.length },
				dependsOn: [
					{ resource: "tasks", identity: ref.identity, relationship: "labels" },
				],
			},
		];
	}),
});
```


Install this as the resource's `derive` function and add `labelCount: number` to `Task` plus `"labelCount"` to the contract's `fields`. A component can then select `"labelCount"` through its usual task query. If you also use the status-label derivation above, combine their returned facts in the one `derive` function.

A relationship source advertises `relationships: ["labels"]` in its capabilities. The response above establishes the actual links.

To derive label names, register a `labels` resource with its own sources and declare the related information needed:


```ts
export const labelNameDependencies: ResourceDefinition["fieldDependencies"] = {
	labelNames(identity, context) {
		const link = { resource: "tasks", identity, relationship: "labels" };
		const labels = context.relationship("tasks", identity, "labels");
		return [
			link,
			...(labels?.targets ?? []).map((target) => ({
				kind: "query" as const,
				resource: target.resource,
				mode: "one" as const,
				identity: target.identity,
				select: ["name"],
			})),
		];
	},
};
```


Vem first acquires unknown links, then reevaluates the prerequisites and chooses reads for the related names alongside all other demand. A corresponding `labelNames` derivation must depend on both the relationship and those name facts; it publishes a value only when the needed evidence is available. The component still selects an ordinary field.

Next: [implement commands and edits](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/commands-edits).

The feature says “complete this task.” The domain definition decides whether to admit that attempt, how to send it, and what the reply proves. Put command definitions in the resource's `commands` object; the names match the public contract.

## Complete a task

This command talks to a synchronous endpoint that returns the updated task:


```ts
complete: {
	access: "public",
	disclosure: "public",
	availability(command, context) {
		const known = context.get("tasks", command.target);
		return known?.values.status === "done" && !known.fields.status?.stale
			? { state: "blocked", reason: "This task is already complete." }
			: { state: "available" };
	},
	project: (command) => [
		{
			resource: "tasks",
			identity: command.target,
			kind: "update",
			values: { status: "done" },
		},
	],
	async run(command, context) {
		context.progress({ message: "Completing task…" });
		const response = await request(
			`/${encodeURIComponent((command.target as TaskRef).id)}/complete`,
			context,
			{ method: "POST" },
		);
		context.observe({ facts: [taskFact(await record(response))] });
		return { kind: "succeeded" };
	},
},
```


`TaskRef` is the contract's `{ id: string }` identity. `request` sends an HTTP request with `context.signal`; `record` checks the status and decodes the returned task; `taskFact` extracts its fields as shown in [connecting a resource](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/resources#declare-sources-and-extract-their-evidence).

The transport helpers used above are small. `ResourceContext` is imported from `@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime`; `TaskRecord` has the task fields plus its ID:


```ts
async function fetchTask(
	path: string,
	context: ResourceContext,
	init?: RequestInit,
) {
	return fetch(`/api/tasks${path}`, { ...init, signal: context.signal });
}
async function record(response: Response): Promise<TaskRecord> {
	if (!response.ok)
		throw new Error(`Task source returned HTTP ${response.status}.`);
	return response.json();
}
```


The definition is built by `createTaskDefinition(request = fetchTask)`, so its `request` calls use this helper unless the host supplies another transport. Keep the helpers beside the resource definition; feature components do not call them.

The three parts have distinct jobs:

- `availability` blocks an attempt when current, non-stale evidence says it is already done. Server authorization still applies.
- `project` supplies a temporary preview. It does not establish success or replace the confirmed value.
- `run` sends the operation, publishes confirmed response facts, and reports its outcome. A status event can update the same facts without extra feature code.

These task examples are public. For protected operations, declare `access` with the write permission and `disclosure` with the permissions needed to show the activity. The [permissions guide](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/access) explains how those expressions compose.

## Report what actually happened

| Evidence | Outcome |
| --- | --- |
| The intended work completed | `succeeded` |
| Some work completed and some did not | `partial`, with a value identifying each result |
| The backend definitively rejected or failed the operation | `failed` |
| The request may have succeeded, but the reply is missing | `unknown` |

The runtime records possible submission before calling `run` when persistence works. The synchronous example holds its submission slot until it settles. `context.submitted()` releases that slot early; it does not prove that the backend accepted the operation. For asynchronous APIs, release that slot when submission is acknowledged and then monitor the accepted operation. Publish meaningful progress with `context.progress`.

A thrown error from a mutation becomes `unknown`: a dropped connection cannot prove that no work occurred. Return a definite failure only when your backend's semantics establish it. `submitted: false` means the request did not reach the backend; it must not be used merely because the response was lost.

If your backend returns an accepted job instead of a completed task, continue to [observe and recover a server operation](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/operations). That guide shows the checkpoint, monitoring, and recovery code separately.


## Declare an HTTP upload

Use `transfer` in place of `run` for a file upload. The definition prepares one request and interprets its receipt; Vem manages submission, progress, cancellation, and recovery on the existing action.

This example adds an attachment to a task. **Its backend contract is illustrative:** `PUT` returns HTTP 204 only after the complete attachment is saved. Add an `upload: defineCommand<{ file: File }, { kind: "succeeded" }>()` entry to the task's public command contract, and install `attachmentUpload(apiOrigin)` as its owner definition:


```ts
import type { CommandDefinition } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
import type { TaskRef } from "../task-board/contract.js";

export function attachmentUpload(apiOrigin: string): CommandDefinition {
	return {
		access: "public",
		disclosure: "public",
		transfer: {
			prepare(command) {
				const target = command.target as TaskRef;
				const { file } = command.input as { file: File };
				return {
					url: new URL(
						`/api/tasks/${encodeURIComponent(target.id)}/attachments/${encodeURIComponent(file.name)}`,
						apiOrigin,
					).href,
					method: "PUT",
					body: file,
					credentials: "same-origin",
				};
			},
			complete(receipt) {
				if (receipt.kind === "unknown") return receipt;
				return receipt.status === 204
					? { kind: "succeeded" }
					: {
							kind: "unknown",
							message:
								"The service did not confirm that the complete attachment was saved.",
						};
			},
		},
	};
}
```


The component binds `useCommand(tasks.commands.upload(target, { file }))` after a file is selected. It does not manage worker messages or a second operation registry. The example is public like the task board; use your application's write permission for `access` and readable activity permission for `disclosure` when the destination is protected.

`prepare` runs for a new attempt. `complete` interprets a captured response and may run again during recovery, so it must not send another mutation. It can publish established facts with `context.observe`. If HTTP acceptance starts a separate server operation, interpret and monitor that operation rather than treating acceptance as completion.

The built-in host supports `POST`, `PUT`, and `PATCH` uploads with `Blob` bodies. Response text is limited to 64 KiB in UTF-8 and response headers to 16 KiB of encoded JSON; an incomplete or oversized response leaves the result unknown. This is for upload receipts, not downloading arbitrary binary files. Upload bytes and request credentials are not copied into the action journal.

[Configure the browser host](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/browser-host#let-the-browser-continue-an-upload) to enable browser-owned execution. Otherwise the owner sends the request and the UI must explain that an application tab needs to remain open. See [recovery limits](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/understand/recovery#uploads-and-background-transfers) before promising resumption or retry.

## Acquire a writable baseline

An editor needs writable values **and the ETag from the same response**. A token from a collection or expanded view may belong to a different representation. Do not assemble a baseline from unrelated field observations and then attach the newest token.

Declare that the detail source supplies an atomic writable representation alongside the ordinary task fields:


```ts
{
	key: "task-detail",
	mode: "one",
	fields: ["title", "status", "notes", "$notesEdit"],
	async run(read, context) {
		const response = await request(
			`/${encodeURIComponent((read.identity as TaskRef).id)}`,
			context,
		);
		return taskResponse(await record(response), response);
	},
},
```


`$notesEdit` is a private domain field containing the response's values and token together. It is absent from the public `Task` contract; feature components still use `useEdit(tasks.edits.notes(target))`. The `taskResponse` helper emits this field only when the response contains an ETag.

<details>
<summary>Extract the values and token from one response</summary>


```ts
interface NotesRepresentation {
	title: string;
	status: Task["status"];
	notes: string;
	etag: string;
}
function taskResponse(
	current: TaskRecord,
	response: Response,
): ObservationBatch {
	const identity = { id: current.id };
	const etag = response.headers.get("ETag");
	return {
		facts: [
			taskFact(current),
			...(etag
				? [
						{
							resource: "tasks",
							identity,
							values: {
								$notesEdit: {
									title: current.title,
									status: current.status,
									notes: current.notes,
									etag,
								} satisfies NotesRepresentation,
							},
							basis: ["public"],
							dependsOn: ["title", "status", "notes"].map((field) => ({
								resource: "tasks",
								identity,
								field,
							})),
						},
					]
				: []),
		],
	};
}
```


The snapshot depends on the fields covered by this backend's token: changing a covered field makes the old snapshot unsupported. This backend's token covers the whole task, even though the editor only changes notes. A backend with a notes-specific token would have different dependencies.

</details>

Now add the edit definition:


```ts
edits: {
	notes: {
		readPrerequisites: (descriptor) => [
			{
				kind: "query",
				resource: "tasks",
				mode: "one",
				identity: descriptor.target,
				select: ["$notesEdit"],
			},
		],
		async prepare(descriptor, context) {
			const writable = context.get("tasks", descriptor.target)?.values
				.$notesEdit as NotesRepresentation | undefined;
			if (!writable)
				throw new Error(
					"The task source omitted its writable representation.",
				);
			return {
				descriptor,
				values: { notes: writable.notes },
				baseline: writable.notes,
				etag: writable.etag,
				disclosure: context.disclosure({
					resource: "tasks",
					identity: descriptor.target,
					field: "$notesEdit",
				}),
				writeAccess: "public",
			};
		},
		command: (prepared, values) => ({
			kind: "command",
			resource: "tasks",
			name: "saveNotes",
			target: prepared.descriptor.target,
			input: { notes: values.notes, etag: prepared.etag },
		}),
	},
},
```


`readPrerequisites` describes the required representation. Vem includes that need in shared acquisition, so concurrent editors and a detail view can reuse the same response. A previously acquired, still-supported snapshot needs no new read. Missing, failed, or denied prerequisites prevent preparation.

`prepare` maps the established snapshot into the editor's baseline. `command` combines the user's current draft with the original token. Incoming changes do not silently replace that token. `context.disclosure` inherits the snapshot's actual permission evidence, so the editor does not reconstruct permission scopes or generations. `writeAccess` controls saving; the save command declares its own admission with `access`. [Permissions](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/access) explains how to protect individual draft fields when they have different disclosure evidence.

## Keep the baseline honest

An event that supplies fresh task fields but no token updates the readable data and marks only `$notesEdit` unsupported. The open editor keeps its original baseline and dirty input. A deliberate reload acquires a new writable representation.

A response can also arrive after a newer event. The task definition compares the snapshot with the latest confirmed fields before allowing a new editor to use it:

<details>
<summary>Reject a snapshot that disagrees with newer confirmed values</summary>


```ts
const writableConsistency: ResourceDefinition["derive"] = (
	changed,
	context,
) => ({
	invalidate: [
		...new Map(
			changed
				.filter((ref) => ref.resource === "tasks")
				.map((ref) => [(ref.identity as TaskRef).id, ref.identity]),
		).values(),
	].flatMap((identity) => {
		const current = context.get("tasks", identity);
		const writable = current?.values.$notesEdit as
			| NotesRepresentation
			| undefined;
		if (!writable || current?.fields.$notesEdit?.stale) return [];
		const matches = ["title", "status", "notes"].every(
			(field) =>
				!current?.fields[field]?.stale &&
				current?.values[field] === writable[field as keyof NotesRepresentation],
		);
		return matches
			? []
			: [{ resource: "tasks", identity, field: "$notesEdit" }];
	}),
});
```


Install this function as `derive: writableConsistency` on the task resource. `dependsOn` handles changes after an established snapshot; this check also handles a late response introducing an already-obsolete snapshot. Neither check starts a periodic verification loop.

</details>

## Save with the original token

The editor above creates `saveNotes`. Add this command next to `complete`:


```ts
saveNotes: {
	access: "public",
	disclosure: "public",
	async run(command, context) {
		const input = command.input as { notes: string; etag: string };
		const response = await request(
			`/${encodeURIComponent((command.target as TaskRef).id)}`,
			context,
			{
				method: "PUT",
				headers: {
					"Content-Type": "application/json",
					"If-Match": input.etag,
				},
				body: JSON.stringify({ notes: input.notes }),
			},
		);
		if (response.status === 412) {
			context.observe({
				invalidate: [
					{
						resource: "tasks",
						identity: command.target,
						field: "$notesEdit",
					},
				],
			});
			return {
				kind: "failed",
				submitted: true,
				error: {
					code: "conflict",
					message:
						"The server notes changed. Your draft is preserved; reload when ready.",
				},
			};
		}
		context.observe(taskResponse(await record(response), response));
		return { kind: "succeeded" };
	},
},
```


The conditional `If-Match` header lets this backend reject an obsolete baseline with HTTP 412. The command reports that conflict without discarding the user's draft and marks the writable snapshot unsupported, even if no event arrived. The component presents a choice to keep editing or discard and reload; it does not manage an independent rollback.

An expanded representation or collection ETag may not be valid for a writable endpoint. Use the token from the representation you will write. If your backend has no conditional writes, document that limitation instead of claiming conflict protection.

Next: [wire permissions](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/access) or [connect the browser host](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/browser-host).

Suppose task titles are public, notes are private, and editing needs another permission. Declare those rules in the domain. Screens still use the same queries, commands, and editors; they do not track callers or clean up protected values themselves.

The server authorizes its requests. Vem governs what the application may attempt, show, and retain.

## 1. Report what the backend permits

Give each permission a stable name. This example uses `tasks:read-notes` and `tasks:write`. Apply decisions after your authentication integration verifies the caller:


```ts
import type { Runtime } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";

interface VerifiedSession {
	subject: string;
	canReadNotes: boolean;
	canEditTasks: boolean;
}
export function applySession(runtime: Runtime, session: VerifiedSession) {
	runtime.setAccess({
		contextId: session.subject,
		decisions: [
			{
				scope: "tasks:read-notes",
				state: session.canReadNotes ? "allowed" : "denied",
			},
			{
				scope: "tasks:write",
				state: session.canEditTasks ? "allowed" : "denied",
			},
		],
	});
}
```


The context ID distinguishes callers on this backend. Establish it from your authentication flow, not from a saved caller ID. A permission can be `allowed`, `denied`, or `unknown`; unlisted permissions start unknown. With persistence, known decisions resume for the same caller after restart. Current host decisions and revocations override saved decisions. A network failure does not establish denial or logout.

## 2. Put read rules beside the resource

Use the same pure definition in the owner and each tab:


```ts
import type { ResourceReadDefinition } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/client";

export const protectedTaskRead: ResourceReadDefinition = {
	name: "tasks",
	access: "public",
	fieldAccess: { notes: "tasks:read-notes" },
};
```


`access` is the common read requirement. `fieldAccess` adds requirements for individual fields. Its map accepts permission expressions directly; a field whose permission depends on its identity can use a function there.

Replace `taskRead` with `protectedTaskRead` in the [resource adapter](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/resources). Vem applies the notes rule to acquisition, projection, stored evidence, and managed drafts. You do not repeat it in each response or component.

A title-and-notes query can still acquire and display the public title when notes are denied. Private filter and sort fields also need permission; they cannot silently influence visible results. Identity must include project or tenant information when that changes a field's permission.

## 3. Describe each operation's requirements

| What needs a rule? | Where it belongs |
| --- | --- |
| A backend read | Source `access` |
| A command attempt | Command `access` |
| Showing an action's details | Command `disclosure` |
| Saving an editor | Baseline `writeAccess` |
| Keeping an editor's readable values | Baseline `disclosure`, inherited from its confirmed representation |

Every command declares its `disclosure` explicitly, including `"public"` for public activity details. Admission and disclosure need not use the same permission. Source and command requirements accept either an expression or a function of their descriptor. Use `{ all: [...] }` when every permission is needed; use `{ any: [...] }` when either permission independently suffices. A common permission is just a string.

For example, a public list can remain available while a private notes source requires `tasks:read-notes`:

<details>
<summary>Implement the separate notes endpoint</summary>

This source assumes `GET /api/tasks/:id/notes` returns `{ notes: string }`. Add it beside the public list in `sources`:


```ts
import type { AcquisitionSource } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
import type { TaskRef } from "../task-board/contract.js";

export const privateNotesSource: AcquisitionSource = {
	key: "private-notes",
	mode: "one",
	fields: ["notes"],
	access: "tasks:read-notes",
	accessUnknown: "wait",
	async run(request, context) {
		const target = request.identity as TaskRef;
		const response = await fetch(
			`/api/tasks/${encodeURIComponent(target.id)}/notes`,
			{ signal: context.signal },
		);
		if (!response.ok)
			throw new Error(`Notes request failed (${response.status}).`);
		const body: { notes: string } = await response.json();
		return {
			facts: [
				{
					resource: "tasks",
					identity: target,
					values: { notes: body.notes },
					basis: ["public"],
				},
			],
		};
	},
};
```


The list advertises only its public fields. The notes source controls whether that request can start; the resource's field rule protects the resulting notes. A source declaration describes capability, not proof that a request succeeded or permission was granted.

</details>

Known denial blocks an attempt. Unknown authority permits a source attempt by default; `accessUnknown: "wait"` waits for an independently established decision. A pure `(request, context) => "wait" | "attempt"` can distinguish caller discovery from a resource permission the endpoint itself establishes. Keep the actual permission in `access`. Use `available` for backend feature support, not authorization.

An editor's `prepare` function inherits the actual writable representation's proof with `context.disclosure({ resource, identity, field })`. The [editor example](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/commands-edits#acquire-a-writable-baseline) shows this mapping. It does not rebuild permission generations. When draft fields have different evidence, `fieldDisclosure` overrides the common proof for those fields.

## 4. Change authority explicitly


```ts
export function withdrawNotes(runtime: Runtime) {
	runtime.revoke("tasks:read-notes");
}
export function leaveAccount(runtime: Runtime) {
	runtime.setAccess({
		contextId: "anonymous",
		decisions: [],
		retirePrevious: true,
	});
}
```


Losing the last read permission forgets the affected facts and managed draft values. Losing only write access disables saving while readable input remains. Changing caller retires that caller's authority; independently permitted information and public consumers survive.

Regrant permits new evidence. It does not restore forgotten values or make a retired response valid again. Keep asynchronous observations attached to their acquisition context. Losing visibility of an action does not prove accepted server work was canceled.

## When the response supplies more specific evidence

Most definitions use the rules above. A response may also reveal an independent invitation, an owning project, or other facts that determine why its information is readable.

<details>
<summary>Combine independent permissions on returned evidence</summary>

`basis` records why a returned fact may be disclosed. The resource's field rule remains an additional requirement. Capture response-specific evidence with `context.proof`:


```ts
import type {
	FactObservation,
	ResourceContext,
} from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
import type { TaskRecord } from "../task-board/domain.js";

export function sharedNotes(
	task: TaskRecord,
	context: ResourceContext,
): FactObservation {
	return {
		resource: "tasks",
		identity: { id: task.id },
		values: { notes: task.notes },
		basis: [
			context.proof({
				all: ["signed-in", { any: ["team-member", "direct-invitation"] }],
			}),
		],
	};
}
```


Here sign-in is required, and either team membership or a direct invitation suffices. Withdrawing team access preserves the notes while the invitation remains valid. Withdrawing sign-in removes them.

A response can establish permissions and information together: return verified `grants` and a symbolic basis such as `basis: [{ all: ["signed-in", "tasks:read-notes"] }]` in the same observation batch. The acquisition context resolves the names after that batch's decisions, while fencing retired authority. Include only grants the response proves.

Entries in the `basis` array are independent alternatives. Memberships and relationships also carry evidence; [per-target relationship permissions](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/events#record-a-relationship-when-links-matter) use `targetBasis`. Use `public` only for permanently readable information. Publicly listed information that can become private needs a revocable permission.

</details>

<details>
<summary>Bind a permission to a project discovered later</summary>

Return this binding in the response's `grants` alongside its facts:


```ts
import type { AccessDecision } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";

export function taskReadBinding(
	taskId: string,
	projectId: string,
): AccessDecision {
	return {
		scope: `tasks:${taskId}:read`,
		requires: {
			all: [`projects:${projectId}:view`, `projects:${projectId}:tasks:view`],
		},
	};
}
```


It declares a dependency, not a grant. Add `state: "allowed"` only if the response verifies permission. Known denial of a prerequisite retires the dependent permission and its evidence. Unknown authority does not revoke existing evidence.

The first binding preserves currently permitted information; changing an established binding retires its old generation. A verified response can establish the new permission and data together. Saved bindings restore before protected payloads become visible. Every persistence adapter implements `writeAccess` for permission snapshots and `writeAccessBindings` for dependencies, even when the binding set is empty. Load both before protected payloads; keep revocation records authoritative over older snapshots. Failures are reported and block further protected payload writes while permitted live data remains usable.

</details>

Try [selective withdrawal](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/understand/access). [Recovery](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/understand/recovery) explains restoration and durable erasure.

This is application setup. Once it is in place, feature components only use the resource contract and hooks.

The **tab** renders UI. A **SharedWorker** runs the domain definitions, makes requests, receives events, and owns shared data. Both use the same pure read rules so filtering already-known data is immediate.

### Three responsibilities

1. Describe what the view needs. Render it with public hooks.
2. Define how backend responses, permissions, and operations work.
3. Connect tabs to one owner, with events and optional storage.

The tab holds a read model for synchronous rendering. The SharedWorker owns acquisition and mutation. Pure read definitions run in both places; credentials and HTTP stay in the owner integration.

## 1. Agree on compatibility

Share this object between your worker and tab modules:


```tsx
import { protocolVersion } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/client";
import { persistenceSchemaVersion } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
export const compatibility = {
	protocol: protocolVersion,
	schema: persistenceSchemaVersion,
	definitions: 1,
	build: "task-board-1",
};
```


Keep `definitions` unchanged for compatible domain edits; increment it when old and new definitions cannot safely share knowledge. `build` identifies the deployment. A different build alone does not require separate owners.

## 2. Start the owner

The worker imports the assembled owner definition as `taskDefinition`. This runnable task service emits full task updates on `task-events`; its facts declare that matching channel. Start with [resource sources](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/resources), then add the event decoder and [commands and edits](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/commands-edits) your backend supports. The shared compatibility object is the one above:


```ts
import { createRuntime, DexiePersistence } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
import { createSharedHost } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/worker";
import { compatibility } from "./compatibility.js";
import { taskDefinition } from "./domain.js";

const attach = createSharedHost({
	compatibility,
	lockName: "vem-task-board-owner",
	locks: navigator.locks,
	async create() {
		const client = createRuntime({
			resources: [taskDefinition],
			persistence: new DexiePersistence("vem-data:task-board:v1"),
		});
		const events = new EventSource("/api/events");
		events.onmessage = (event) => {
			try {
				client.event("tasks", JSON.parse(event.data));
			} catch (error) {
				console.error("Invalid task event", error);
			}
		};
		await new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
			events.onopen = () => {
				client.setContinuity({ channel: "task-events" }, "live");
				resolve();
			};
			events.onerror = () => {
				client.setContinuity({ channel: "task-events" }, "interrupted");
				resolve();
			};
		});
		return {
			client,
			dispose() {
				events.close();
				client.close();
			},
		};
	},
});
const scope = globalThis as unknown as {
	onconnect: (event: MessageEvent) => void;
};
scope.onconnect = (event) => {
	for (const port of event.ports) void attach.then((connect) => connect(port));
};
```


`/api/events` is this example's backend event stream. Adapt its URL and event decoder to your service. This fixture sends full task updates. `setContinuity` reports whether that channel is continuously delivering them; a normal incoming update goes through `client.event`.

The starter waits for the initial event connection to open or fail before admitting readers. This keeps a late startup coverage change from making the first request obsolete. On failure, readers can still acquire snapshots. Reconnecting does not make observations from the uncovered interval current; affected active consumers need new evidence. Give the event transport a connection deadline if it can remain silent indefinitely.

Publish the worker at a stable URL, such as `/task-owner.js`, and keep the lock name stable across deployments. Changing the lock name for each release could allow two owners to run at once.

## 3. Connect the tab and provide the client

Pass the same `taskRead` rules used by the owner. `ownerModule` is the URL of the compiled worker module, served from the application's origin.


```tsx
import { connectSharedWorker } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/client";
import { taskRead } from "../task-board/read.js";

export function connectTasks(ownerModule: URL) {
	return connectSharedWorker(ownerModule, compatibility, {
		readDefinitions: [taskRead],
	});
}
```


Create the client once in the application entry point, then pass it into this provider. The browser needs HTTPS or loopback, SharedWorker, and Web Locks. Connection failures are application-level errors, separate from an individual query's failure.

A slow worker download, owner-lock wait, or host initialization keeps `connectionSnapshot().phase` at `connecting`. Elapsed time alone does not fail the connection; a late owner can still become ready. Worker errors, rejected initialization, and incompatible versions report an actual failure. Keep the application's connecting state visible, and let users reconnect or leave. An imperative read can stop waiting through its `AbortSignal` without closing the shared client. The host waits for the owner client's optional `ready` promise and delivers its initial knowledge snapshot before admitting queued demand. Restored rows can therefore supply nested consumers before their requirements are planned together. Give backend operations their own documented deadlines; the client handshake does not infer one.


```tsx
import type { BrowserClient } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/client";
import { DataProvider } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/react";
import { type ReactNode, useSyncExternalStore } from "react";

export function TaskProvider({
	client,
	children,
}: {
	client: BrowserClient;
	children: ReactNode;
}) {
	const connection = useSyncExternalStore(
		client.subscribe,
		client.connectionSnapshot,
	);
	if (connection.phase === "connecting") return <p>Connecting…</p>;
	if (connection.phase !== "ready") {
		return (
			<p role="alert">{connection.error ?? "Reconnect to the task service."}</p>
		);
	}
	return <DataProvider client={client}>{children}</DataProvider>;
}
```


Put your routes inside the provider. Close the client when the app is permanently disposed; do not recreate it on route changes.

## Decide what survives a restart

Storage is optional: omit `persistence` for an in-memory owner. With `DexiePersistence`, a definition's `persist` decides which fields can be saved. For example, the task definition allows `$presence`, `title`, and `status`, and leaves private notes out. Relationships have a separate `persistRelationship` allowance. The starter uses the explicit IndexedDB name `vem-data:task-board:v1`; the constructor uses that name directly. Choose a namespace your application owns.

`runtime.ready` means restoration was attempted. Establish the current caller through [access decisions](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/access); storage must not choose the caller. Until then, protected saved data stays quarantined. Identifying the same caller resumes saved permissions and exposes supported stale values immediately, so mounted consumers can express their current needs together. Acquiring fresh values does not require clearing readable old ones.

`DexiePersistence` retains permission snapshots for up to 16 caller contexts; evicted snapshots require new permission evidence. The storage format upgrades automatically. Report persistence errors through `RuntimeOptions.onError`, and inspect `action.recovery` before promising restart recovery.

## Let the browser continue an upload

This optional host integration uses Background Fetch where available. Commands declare their HTTP transfer; feature components keep using `useCommand` and `useAction`. Configure three cooperating pieces once per application:

| Where | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| SharedWorker | Runs the action, prepares the request, and interprets its result |
| Page | Initiates the browser transfer after the ServiceWorker admits the handoff |
| ServiceWorker | Atomically claims the protected action and stores its completion receipt |

The owner and ServiceWorker must use the **same Dexie database** as the action journal. All three use the same channel name. Keep both names stable across compatible deployments:


```ts
export const transferHost = {
	databaseName: "vem-data:task-board:v1",
	channel: "task-board-transfers-v1",
};
```


**In the SharedWorker**, use this factory from the existing `createSharedHost({ create })` callback. Retain your domain event connection and close it in the same `dispose` function:


```ts
import {
	createRuntime,
	DexiePersistence,
	type ResourceDefinition,
} from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
import { createBackgroundTransfers } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/worker";
import { transferHost } from "./transfer-config.js";

export function createUploadOwner(resources: readonly ResourceDefinition[]) {
	const transfers = createBackgroundTransfers(transferHost);
	const client = createRuntime({
		resources,
		persistence: new DexiePersistence(transferHost.databaseName),
		transfers,
	});
	return {
		client,
		dispose() {
			client.close();
			transfers.close();
		},
	};
}
```


**In the application entry point**, call `connectUploadHost()` once. Keep the returned cleanup function for permanent application disposal:


```ts
import { attachBackgroundTransfers } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/client";
import { transferHost } from "./transfer-config.js";

export function connectUploadHost() {
	return attachBackgroundTransfers({
		serviceWorker: "/transfer-service-worker.js",
		scope: "/",
		channel: transferHost.channel,
	});
}
```


**In `/transfer-service-worker.js`**, compiled from this module:


```ts
import { installBackgroundTransfers } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/worker";
import { transferHost } from "./transfer-config.js";

installBackgroundTransfers(transferHost);
```


Serve this worker from your own origin with an appropriate scope. The example uses the origin root; an application under a subpath should use that subpath for both URL and scope. If the application already registers a ServiceWorker, install the handlers in that worker instead of creating a competing registration. This integration does not install a cache or intercept ordinary requests.

The native host accepts HTTP `POST`, `PUT`, and `PATCH` uploads with a `Blob` body and a small text response. The [transfer definition](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/commands-edits#declare-an-http-upload) describes the backend contract. Browser support, storage, and permission can prevent handoff; display the action's actual [execution lifetime](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/understand/recovery#uploads-and-background-transfers), not a promise inferred from API availability.

<details>
<summary>Handle an application update</summary>

A compatible client can report `connection.updateAvailable`. To offer an update button:


```tsx
export async function updateApp(client: BrowserClient) {
	const update = await client.upgrade();
	if (update.ready) location.reload();
	return update.reason;
}
```


Display the returned reason if the owner refuses, for example because work is active or storage could not be flushed. An incompatible connection cannot use the old owner; ask the user to finish work in older tabs and close them before reopening. Never bypass this by inventing another lock name.

</details>

Next: [move progress from the page into shared feedback](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/feedback).

A task is still completing when its page closes. Its progress should move into a notice, then return inline if the page opens again. The owner decides which tab should show that notice; the application decides how it looks.

## Mark the inline surface

Use a shared page component to claim feedback for its query. The effect returns the release function, so navigating away automatically ends the claim.


```tsx
import type { BrowserClient } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/client";
import { useQuery } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/react";
import { useEffect } from "react";
import { tasks } from "../task-board/contract.js";

export function TaskPageProgress({ client }: { client: BrowserClient }) {
	const result = useQuery(tasks.list({ select: ["title", "status"] }));
	useEffect(
		() =>
			client.claimQueryFeedback(tasks.list({ select: ["title", "status"] })),
		[client],
	);
	return (
		<div aria-live="polite">
			{result.activities.map((action) => (
				<p key={action.id}>
					{action.outcome?.kind ?? action.progress?.message ?? "Working…"}
				</p>
			))}
		</div>
	);
}
```


Mount this alongside the task list while the route is open. A scoped page should claim that same scope. Individual action buttons do not need to manage ownership.

## Render the shell's notices

Mount this component once inside `DataProvider`, outside the route content. `useActivities` reads shared action records; `client.feedback()` returns the IDs assigned to this tab for unsolicited feedback.


```tsx
import { useActivities } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/react";
import { useSyncExternalStore } from "react";

export function ActionNotices({ client }: { client: BrowserClient }) {
	const ids = useSyncExternalStore(client.subscribe, client.feedback);
	const activities = useActivities();
	return (
		<aside aria-label="Activity" aria-live="polite">
			{activities
				.filter((action) => ids.includes(action.id))
				.map((action) => (
					<div key={action.id}>
						<span>
							{action.outcome?.kind ?? action.progress?.message ?? "Working…"}
						</span>
						<button
							type="button"
							onClick={() => client.acknowledgeFeedback(action.id, action)}
						>
							Dismiss
						</button>
					</div>
				))}
		</aside>
	);
}
```


This deliberately plain renderer shows the wiring. Replace its markup with your toast component and map outcome names to your application's messages.

Pass the displayed action snapshot to `acknowledgeFeedback`. That acknowledges the stage the user saw: dismissing a running notice will not accidentally dismiss a later failure.

## What happens in another tab?

The tab that started the action receives unsolicited feedback. If it closes, a surviving connected tab takes responsibility. Other tabs may still display related progress inline. Dismissal changes presentation; it never cancels accepted server work.

For a surface that has action IDs rather than a query, `claimFeedback(ids)` provides the same claim/release lifecycle. Test navigation and closing the originating tab in your application's browser tests.

**Vem chooses the reads. You describe what each source can provide.** There is no planner to write for a screen, resource, or combination of tabs.

A source is one supported way to read your backend: a URL list, a summary collection, one full record, a filtered search, or a page. It declares what information that read can establish and how to execute it.

## Plan for the whole application

The shared owner combines the currently outstanding query requirements it knows about, from every connected tab and imperative reader. It subtracts permitted information already known, expands prerequisites for derived fields, and compares combinations of available sources. Work already in flight can satisfy overlapping demand without another request. Later arrivals can reuse that work and its results; Vem does not predict requests from a tab that has not asked yet.

The browser client groups subscriptions and reads made in the same synchronous turn before handing them to the owner. A set of newly mounted rows can therefore participate in one planning decision. Render permitted stale rows while they revalidate: their mounted consumers add their field requirements immediately. This also works after restart once the host identifies the same caller and saved permissions resume; no consumer history or fetch hints are needed.

For example, four task details might be cheaper as one collection read. If three details are already known, one detail read might be cheaper. If another tab also needs collection membership, that changes the comparison again. No component chooses an endpoint.

### Experiment: One plan for several views

Open all task notes, then compare a single note after starting over. Explore sharing a pending reply and filtered pages.

Vem compares declared sources across the views that currently need information. Three task-note views can share a cheaper collection read; one view can use a detail read. A second view can join an in-flight fixed representation. A supported filter narrows acquisition, and the runtime follows pages until the actual response establishes completeness. The receipt counts real source calls to the simulated service.

Runs a deterministic simulated source with the real Vem runtime. Interactive controls are available in the HTML page; no JavaScript is needed to read this explanation.

The receipt shows calls made by the runtime. The task views only ask for fields; none chooses the source.

### From need to knowledge

1. Collect what active consumers across tabs still need.
2. Subtract known facts and account for work already in flight.
3. Choose compatible reads using coverage, permissions, and cost.
4. Publish actual evidence, then plan for any remaining gaps.

The shared owner combines demand from tabs and imperative readers, subtracts known information, and reuses in-flight work. It compares declared backend capabilities and costs. Responses establish facts; missing information may require another source.

## Describe the alternatives

These two backend functions return observation batches, as shown in [Connect a resource](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/resources). Their source declarations expose the choice to Vem:


```ts
import type {
	AcquisitionSource,
	ObservationBatch,
	ResourceContext,
} from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";

interface TaskReads {
	all(context: ResourceContext): Promise<ObservationBatch>;
	one(id: string, context: ResourceContext): Promise<ObservationBatch>;
}
export function taskSources(api: TaskReads): readonly AcquisitionSource[] {
	return [
		{
			key: "all-tasks",
			mode: "list",
			fields: ["title", "status", "notes"],
			membership: true,
			run: (_request, context) => api.all(context),
		},
		{
			key: "one-task",
			mode: "one",
			fields: ["title", "status", "notes"],
			run: (request, context) =>
				api.one((request.identity as { id: string }).id, context),
		},
	];
}
```


`key` names a backend source and must be unique within its resource. It is not a query cache key: Vem derives concrete request and query identities automatically. Screens do not define keys.

Register them as `sources: taskSources(api)` on the resource definition. Neither source needs a cost declaration. Both return fixed representations: one full task or all full tasks. Vem can use every returned field, even if the first consumer did not select it.

## Choose using available evidence

Vem compares combinations of reads for the outstanding requirements. Its default policy prefers bounded acquisition, then the smallest total response across the chosen reads. When totals match, it favors fewer requests, then less information outside the current requirements. Response size counts returned identities and advertised values, including duplicates across requests; it does not estimate bytes, latency, or server processing.

| Outstanding need | Default choice | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Descriptions for all 5 known tasks | Collection | The same five representations in one request |
| Descriptions for 3 of 40 known tasks | Details | Avoid returning 37 unrelated representations |
| Names with no known inventory | Name list | Establish the choices using the narrower representation |
| Several known task details, unknown collection size | Details | Their response size is bounded; the full collection's is not |

The planner considers all consumers together. A list needing 500 names does not also need 500 descriptions: visible rows add those requirements separately. In a filtered list, display fields are needed only for established matches; an unresolved candidate first needs evidence that decides whether it belongs. Already-known descriptions need no read, and shared responses can avoid duplicating fields across requests. Another view asking for membership or fields can therefore change the choice; there is no fixed “fetch the collection after three rows” threshold.

Population information comes from permitted knowledge for the source's actual scope and supported filter:

- **Known:** the observed inventory establishes the number of matches.
- **Bounded:** a complete inventory is available, but some candidates cannot yet be matched against the filter.
- **Unknown:** incomplete information supplies no upper bound.

A retained complete inventory remains useful after an event gap or restart. Its count is historical planning evidence, not a guarantee about the current collection. Vem does not substitute an invented population when information is missing. A partial inventory cannot establish an upper bound, and withdrawn knowledge cannot support the estimate. None of these estimates creates presentation rows or proves current completeness.

Planning uses a bounded search and keeps the best combination it finds. This policy reduces avoidable reads without claiming a universally optimal plan for unknown backends.

<details>
<summary>When the backend supplies a meaningful cost model</summary>

A service may publish read charges or expose a validated work model. In that case, every alternative source for the resource must declare comparable `cost` values. Do not mix estimated alternatives with unpriced ones.

This example accepts documented request and returned-item charges from the integration:


```ts
export function meteredTaskSources(
	api: TaskReads,
	charges: { perRequest: number; perItem: number },
): readonly AcquisitionSource[] {
	return taskSources(api).map((source) => ({
		...source,
		cost: { request: charges.perRequest, perItem: charges.perItem },
	}));
}
```


An object cost is **request charge × request count + item charge × returned-item count**. Vem supplies the population estimate and accounts for continuation requests. Unknown collection size stays unknown; `maxPages` is an execution limit, not evidence of collection size.

A numeric cost describes the whole acquisition, including continuation. A pure `cost(request, context)` callback can return a number or cost object when the model depends on backend capabilities or the requested projection. Callbacks must not fetch, publish observations, change permission, or keep hidden state.

Costs are supplied by the integration; Vem does not learn them from request timings. Elapsed time alone does not measure server work. Ordinary source definitions should use the default policy rather than inventing weights.

</details>

## Cooperate with browser scheduling

Vem starts independent selected reads without a default concurrency ceiling. It still combines overlapping demand before dispatch and respects dependencies. The browser manages connections and HTTP multiplexing; Vem does not assume six available connections.

A source receives a derived `context.fetchPriority`: missing information uses `high`, while revalidation supported by readable stale evidence uses `low`. Shared work takes the strongest demand, and prerequisites inherit the need they unlock. Sources without classified demand use `auto`. Pass the hint through your shared transport to `fetch(url, { signal: context.signal, priority: context.fetchPriority })`; screens do not declare priorities. Preparing an editor uses `high`.

These are browser hints, not ordering guarantees. New consumers can change the hint for subsequent page requests, but Vem does not cancel and repeat a request to change a priority already submitted. An optional host `maxConcurrent` can constrain specific backends; its waiting work retains arrival order. Command admission has separate limits.

Once replacement reads are running, Vem aborts a redundant read only when the selected replacements cover every remaining requirement it serves across consumers. Scope, filters, freshness, and representation-specific fields still matter: a collection response cannot replace a detail-only ETag. Canceled reads cannot publish late results, and a failed or partial replacement can fall back to the canceled source. This stops browser observation and transfer; it cannot guarantee that the server stopped processing the request.

Transport deadlines include time waiting for the browser to obtain a connection and receive the response. Fetch does not expose a socket-assignment event that can safely start a server-only timer. Choose an end-to-end deadline for the application, preserve readable content if it expires, and let the query report failure and offer retry. A timeout alone establishes neither server processing time nor loss of permission.

## Which characteristics belong in the definition?

| Capability | What Vem uses it for |
| --- | --- |
| `fields`, `relationships` | Identify which missing facts or links a read can supply |
| `membership` | Distinguish enumeration from details about already known records |
| `scope` | Keep tenant, project, or other collection boundaries separate |
| `filters` | Push compatible predicates to the backend; evaluate remaining requirements locally |
| `select: true` | Request only missing fields when the backend supports field selection |
| `cost` | Optionally supply a justified, comparable backend work model |
| `pagination` | Follow bounded continuation without claiming an early page is complete |
| `access`, `accessUnknown` | Admit reads according to current authority before dispatch |
| `available` | Exclude a representation the backend does not support |

By default a source returns a fixed representation. With `select: true`, `request.fields` is the requested projection. Vem cannot expand a request already sent; a later field may require additional work. A fixed representation already in flight can serve any of its declared fields.

A URL, response header, or nested reference can establish facts too. Extract and advertise those facts when the backend semantics justify them. A URL containing a task ID may establish that ID; it does not establish permission, notes, or a writable representation's ETag.

<details>
<summary>When a collection returns only task URLs</summary>

Suppose a public endpoint returns the full list of URLs such as `/api/tasks/walk`. `load` below performs that request and checks its response. The task contract puts `id` in its resource reference, so this source can establish membership and presence without any display fields:


```ts
export function taskUrlSource(
	load: (signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<readonly string[]>,
): AcquisitionSource {
	return {
		key: "task-links",
		mode: "list",
		fields: [],
		membership: true,
		async run(_request, context) {
			const identities = (await load(context.signal)).map((href) => {
				const path = new URL(href, "https://tasks.example").pathname;
				const match = /^\/api\/tasks\/([^/]+)$/.exec(path);
				if (!match?.[1]) throw new Error("Unexpected task URL");
				return { id: decodeURIComponent(match[1]) };
			});
			return {
				facts: identities.map((identity) => ({
					resource: "tasks",
					identity,
					presence: "present",
					basis: ["public"],
				})),
				memberships: [
					{
						resource: "tasks",
						identities,
						complete: true,
						basis: ["public"],
					},
				],
			};
		},
	};
}
```


Add it alongside the other sources. Vem can use these known identities when choosing detail reads; this response supplies no title, status, or notes. If your contract also exposes an identity-derived field such as `name`, advertise it in `fields` and return its verified value in `values`.

This example's `complete: true` relies on the endpoint returning the whole visible collection. Use incomplete membership for partial responses. Preserve project or tenant context in both identity and scope when the URLs belong to separate collections.

</details>

## Support backend filters

A feature's [text query](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/build/display#filter-text) describes its intended matches. To let Vem use a backend search endpoint, assign its supported semantics to the source's `filters`:


```ts
import type { FilterCapabilities } from "@hyecompany/vem-data";

export const taskSearchFilters = {
	fields: ["title", "notes"],
	operators: ["equal", "contains"],
	combinations: ["all", "any"],
	text: [{ case: "insensitive", accents: "sensitive" }],
} satisfies FilterCapabilities;
```


Use `filters: taskSearchFilters` on that source. Vem normalizes a feature's `where` object into a predicate before planning. `run` receives the supported predicate in `request.where`; your transport encodes it in the backend's syntax. `isFilterExpression` distinguishes that normalized expression from an equality map. Expressions expose `$filter`, with `field` and `value` on leaves, `comparison` on text leaves, and `conditions` on `all`/`any` groups. Feature code uses `where` objects; only source adapters need this representation.

Declare only behavior the backend actually supports. The `text` combinations must match exactly; a case-insensitive query cannot use a case-sensitive backend search. Omit `text` when the source cannot search substrings. An optional pure `supports(expression, context)` can further restrict supported grouping or server capabilities. The existing `filters: ["status"]` shorthand declares equality support only.

An OR (`any`) needs support for **all its branches** before Vem can send it to that source. Searching titles alone would lose tasks matching only notes. Unsupported predicates therefore use broader reads and local evaluation; an AND (`all`) may still use supported conditions to narrow those reads safely. If no source can supply enough evidence, the result settles with unresolved candidates instead of inventing matches.

A source can return only matching URLs or identities. Report those identities as membership for the **actual predicate sent**, with the actual scope and completeness. This proves that each returned task matched the whole expression; it does not reveal which OR branch matched or invent the text of its notes.

<details>
<summary>Record matching identities without inventing fields</summary>

After the transport validates the response and extracts task references from its URLs, this helper records a public search result. Pass `request.where`, the returned identities, and the response's established completeness; include `scope` too for a scoped resource.


```ts
import type {
	AcquisitionRequest,
	ObservationBatch,
} from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";
import type { TaskRef } from "../task-board/contract.js";

export function taskSearchMembership(
	where: AcquisitionRequest["where"],
	identities: readonly TaskRef[],
	complete: boolean,
): ObservationBatch {
	return {
		memberships: [
			{
				resource: "tasks",
				where,
				identities,
				complete,
				basis: ["public"],
			},
		],
	};
}
```


Return this batch from the source's `run`. A public basis is appropriate only for a public endpoint; use the response's actual [disclosure permissions](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/access) for protected search results. Publish any independently established fields as facts alongside membership.

</details>

Equality can prove more: membership for `status: "open"` establishes that value even when it is omitted from each record. A complete filtered result still does not establish the whole unfiltered collection. Filtering may reduce returned data without reducing a server's scan or computation. Only an explicit backend work model can account for that difference.

## Filtering and pages

This source accepts a status filter and cursor pagination. The transport performs **one page**; Vem owns continuation:


```ts
interface TaskPage {
	tasks: Array<{ id: string; title: string; status: string }>;
	next?: string;
	complete: boolean;
}
export function pagedTasks(
	load: (url: URL, signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<TaskPage>,
): AcquisitionSource {
	return {
		key: "task-pages",
		mode: "list",
		fields: ["title", "status"],
		membership: true,
		filters: ["status"],
		pagination: { pageSize: 50, maxPages: 20 },
		async run(request, context) {
			const url = new URL("/api/tasks", "https://tasks.example");
			if (request.where?.status)
				url.searchParams.set("status", String(request.where.status));
			url.searchParams.set("limit", String(request.page?.limit));
			if (request.page?.cursor)
				url.searchParams.set("cursor", String(request.page.cursor));
			const page = await load(url, context.signal);
			return {
				batch: {
					facts: page.tasks.map(({ id, ...values }) => ({
						resource: "tasks",
						identity: { id },
						values,
						presence: "present",
						basis: ["public"],
					})),
					memberships: [
						{
							resource: "tasks",
							where: request.where,
							identities: page.tasks.map(({ id }) => ({ id })),
							complete: page.complete,
							basis: ["public"],
						},
					],
				},
				next: page.next,
			};
		},
	};
}
```


`load` is your HTTP transport. `TaskPage.complete` reports whether the service established the final collection, including any partial-service failures. Vem accumulates page identities and keeps membership incomplete while continuation remains. A missing cursor is not, by itself, proof of completeness. Repeated cursors and page limits stop acquisition with an explanation; partial rows stay useful.

Pages may have different disclosure permissions. The accumulated collection is complete only while every contributing page’s evidence remains permitted; withdrawing a page’s permission preserves independently readable facts but removes that complete-set claim. Continuity combines across pages too: a complete set relies on every contributing page’s change coverage, while individual identities retain their own page’s support.

## Prerequisites and partial answers

Some reads require information first: for example, service capabilities determine which task representation exists. Declare that requirement with `readPrerequisites`:


```ts
import type { ResourceDefinition } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime";

export const taskReadPrerequisites: ResourceDefinition["readPrerequisites"] =
	() => [
		{
			kind: "query",
			resource: "service",
			mode: "one",
			identity: "current",
			select: ["features"],
		},
	];
```


Install this as `readPrerequisites: taskReadPrerequisites` and register a `service` definition whose sources supply `features`. Vem shares that prerequisite acquisition across consumers, then chooses task sources from the updated knowledge. Already satisfied task queries keep using their data; this declaration does not introduce verification on each read. Failed, denied, or still-incomplete prerequisites settle with an explanation instead of dispatching the guarded source or retrying indefinitely.

Use `fieldDependencies` when a derived value needs other fields or related resources. These supply an alternative way to obtain a value, alongside sources that return it directly. It returns descriptions of the required information, using known identities or relationships; it does not fetch or choose endpoints. Vem includes those prerequisites in the shared plan and reevaluates them as evidence arrives. The existing `derive` function computes the value and reports its evidence dependencies. See [relationships and derived values](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/events).

A parent response can also establish another resource's existence or collection. Suppose a task detail includes its complete checklist. The checklist resource can declare a task-detail query through `membershipDependencies` for its list, or `presenceDependencies` for one item. Vem compares that route with direct checklist sources and can share a task read already needed elsewhere. The task response's extraction must still publish the checklist facts and scoped membership; a dependency declaration alone proves nothing. These callbacks return the same query or relationship descriptions as `fieldDependencies`, without orchestrating requests.

A source's declaration is a capability, not evidence. If a response omits fields or contains only part of a collection, publish exactly that. Vem can try another eligible source, then settles with the remaining gaps. It does not repeatedly request an unavailable field until it appears. Retry begins a new attempt; ordinary data has no time-based expiry.

Next: [connect permissions](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/access), or [apply events without unnecessary reads](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/events).

## Classify a rejected read

A source normally throws an ordinary error when acquisition fails. If the backend establishes a more specific result, throw `AcquisitionError` from `@hyecompany/vem-data/runtime` with a `QueryIssue`, such as `{ kind: "access", message: "Listing is not permitted." }`. Consumers receive that classification alongside any still-readable content and can explicitly retry.

This describes the failed attempt. It does not revoke authentication, remove independently permitted facts, or permanently deny future attempts. Use the [access model](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/access) separately when the backend provides evidence that disclosure permission has been withdrawn. Late classified failures use the same access-generation fence as other acquisition failures.

Use **live inspection** to understand what a mounted screen requests. Use **scenarios** to see how that same screen handles missing data, denied access, or a failed command. Feature components use their usual queries, commands, and editors in either mode.

## Mount the inspector

Vem UI's `DataInspector` shows discovered queries, commands, editors, and activities. Put it in your existing developer-tools surface, such as TanStack Devtools:


```tsx
import type {
	ClientInspector,
	ScenarioClient,
} from "@hyecompany/vem-data/devtools";
import { DataInspector } from "@hyecompany/vem-ui/devtools";
import "@hyecompany/vem-ui/styles.css";
import "@hyecompany/vem-ui/devtools/styles.css";

export function InspectorPanel({
	inspection,
	scenarios,
}: {
	inspection: ClientInspector;
	scenarios?: ScenarioClient;
}) {
	return <DataInspector inspection={inspection} scenarios={scenarios} />;
}
```


The panel accepts an existing inspector; it does not create or close clients or request data. The optional `header` prop holds application-specific connection information or mode controls. Both stylesheets are required.

For live inspection, wrap the application's client with `inspectClient`:


```tsx
import type { DataClient } from "@hyecompany/vem-data";
import { inspectClient } from "@hyecompany/vem-data/devtools";

export function prepareDevelopmentTools(client: DataClient) {
	const inspection = inspectClient(client);
	return {
		client: inspection.client,
		panel: <InspectorPanel inspection={inspection} />,
		dispose: () => inspection.close(),
	};
}
```


Pass the returned `client` to `DataProvider` and `panel` to your developer-tools host. Only consumers using the wrapper appear in the inspection. The host calls `dispose()` when retiring this development session; it detaches inspection without closing the real client. Closing the visible panel alone need not retire the session.

The inspector shows consumer demand and results, not owner-side source comparisons. See [how Vem chooses reads](https://hyevem.com/docs/data/connect/planning#choose-using-available-evidence) for the selection policy; service or network observations identify the actual reads.

